1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,899
Why can one program read a file, and another cannot? Often the answer is a label.

2
00:00:05,919 --> 00:00:09,511
SELinux puts a label on every file and every process.

3
00:00:09,531 --> 00:00:11,964
Today we learn to read those labels.

4
00:00:12,434 --> 00:00:17,299
In this video: list and identify SELinux file and process context.

5
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:24,402
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:24,872 --> 00:00:28,649
List and identify SELinux file and process context.

7
00:00:29,119 --> 00:00:34,240
We'll read the context of files, of directories, of processes, and of our own login.

8
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:45,595
Every file and every process has an SELinux context:

9
00:00:45,615 --> 00:00:49,969
a label. SELinux compares labels to decide what is allowed.

10
00:00:49,989 --> 00:00:55,603
A context has four fields, separated by colons: user, role, type, and level.

11
00:00:55,623 --> 00:00:59,532
For the exam, the third field matters most: the type.

12
00:01:07,002 --> 00:01:11,673
Three commands show it, and they share one option: a capital Z.

13
00:01:11,693 --> 00:01:16,364
ls with capital Z, for files. ps with capital Z, for processes.

14
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:18,673
And id with capital Z, for you.

15
00:01:27,193 --> 00:01:31,648
Start with files. ls with capital Z adds the context to the listing.

16
00:01:31,668 --> 00:01:36,386
For passwd, the type is passwd file t. For shadow, the type is shadow t.

17
00:01:36,406 --> 00:01:41,914
Different files, different types: that is how the policy can treat them differently.

18
00:01:47,434 --> 00:01:51,277
Directories work the same way. The option dash d means:

19
00:01:51,297 --> 00:01:54,368
the directory itself, not what is inside it.

20
00:01:54,388 --> 00:01:57,389
Slash var slash log has the type var log t.

21
00:01:57,409 --> 00:02:01,814
And the home directory of student has the type user home dir t.

22
00:02:06,334 --> 00:02:11,549
Processes have a context too. ps with capital Z adds the label in front.

23
00:02:11,569 --> 00:02:16,201
The option dash capital C selects a process by its command name.

24
00:02:16,221 --> 00:02:19,255
The ssh service runs with the type sshd t.

25
00:02:19,275 --> 00:02:22,454
The cron service runs with the type crond t.

26
00:02:22,474 --> 00:02:28,415
Each service has its own type, and that is how SELinux limits it to what it needs.

27
00:02:32,935 --> 00:02:36,701
And you? id with capital Z shows your own context:

28
00:02:36,721 --> 00:02:39,805
unconfined u, unconfined r, unconfined t.

29
00:02:39,825 --> 00:02:44,652
Unconfined means the policy puts almost no limits on this login.

30
00:02:44,672 --> 00:02:50,558
ps with capital Z alone lists your own processes, and they share your context.

31
00:02:55,078 --> 00:02:59,879
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot when the task needs one, and your turn.

32
00:03:01,399 --> 00:03:07,762
Your turn. In your home directory, write a file named context dot t x t, with two lines.

33
00:03:07,782 --> 00:03:12,404
Line one: the type of the file slash etc slash shadow. Line two:

34
00:03:12,424 --> 00:03:18,062
the type of the sshd process. Only the type, the third field. Run the checker.

35
00:03:28,532 --> 00:03:30,282
Try before you open the solution.

36
00:03:35,802 --> 00:03:38,406
Next up: restore default file contexts.

37
00:03:38,876 --> 00:03:41,779
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
